  "body": "Parent: [#6021](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/6021) (classification revalidation scan). Research source: prior-art doc `~/workspace/scratch/atlas-ingestion-brief-2026-11-04/prior-art-research.md` (2026-11-04). Follow-up 30 (Cluster 5).\n\n## Finding (research, in substance)\n\nOne-time scanning certifies a moment, not a corpus — that is a named dead end. Rules improve, corpora get re-tiered, and sources turn out dirtier than believed, so **revalidation is a recurring gate**: new rules find old leaks. The cadence design has three moving parts:\n\n- **Rule-update-triggered rescans:** when the scanner rules / inventory pack / recognizer set changes, the affected scan re-runs over the corpus (deterministic layer in full; NER layer per its scoping design, sibling under #6021).\n- **Sampled human audit per round:** a small random sample of each tier hand-checked, to *estimate the scanners' miss rate* instead of assuming it. \"No detector catches everything\" is every tool's own disclaimer; the audit sample turns that caveat into a number.\n- **Allowlist expiry:** allowlist entries carry reasons and expiry; a stale allowlist is how leaks get grandfathered.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis design takes #6021's recurrence recommendation (one-off vs standing gate) as its input and specifies the standing form: triggers, per-layer scope per trigger, audit sample sizes, and where the cadence plugs into the ingestion-operations design (#6019) as a standing gate.\n\n## Done looks like\n\nA cadence spec: trigger table, scope per trigger, audit method with miss-rate estimate, allowlist lifecycle.\n\n<!-- agent-note:v1 agent=muse state=filed source=main-chat-2026-11-04-wave2 -->",
